    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I did.\n\n## Changes\n\nThe spec requires all 400/409/429 rejections to return an RFC 7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status` and `detail`. Two issues stood between the code and that requirement:\n\n**1. A latent NPE that turned a 400 into a 500 (`LocalityResolver.java`)**\n\nThe new acceptance test posts an owner with `city` removed and expects a `400` problem+json. `city` is already `@NotNull @NotBlank`, but during the same validation pass the class-level `@ValidPostcode` validator ran `PostcodeRule.isValid(null, ...)` \u2192 `LocalityResolver.resolve(null)` \u2192 `Map.of(...).getOrDefault(null, ...)`. Immutable maps reject `null` keys, so this threw an NPE, which Hibernate Validator wrapped as a `ValidationException` and the generic handler reported as **500**.\n\nThe class's own Javadoc promises `UNKNOWN` for a null city, so I guarded `resolve` to honor that contract. With the NPE gone, the missing `city` surfaces as the normal `@NotNull`/`@NotBlank` violation \u2192 `MethodArgumentNotValidException` \u2192 the existing handler's `ProblemDetail` (400, problem+json).\n\n**2. Business-rule rejections returned bare statuses with no body (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)**\n\nThe four business rejections in `addOwner` returned `new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.\u2026)` \u2014 a 400/429/409 with an empty body, violating the spec. I:\n\n- Added a focused `BusinessRuleViolationException` carrying the HTTP status, a short `title`, and a `detail`.\n- Added `handleBusinessRuleViolationException` to `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, which reuses the existing `detailBuild(...)` helper (same `ProblemDetail` machinery, `type`/`status`/`detail`/timestamp) and sets the rule-specific `title`.\n- Replaced the four bare returns with throws carrying meaningful titles/details (future registration date \u2192 400, daily limit \u2192 429, city at capacity \u2192 409, duplicate owner \u2192 409).\n\nThe dedicated `@ExceptionHandler` takes precedence over the generic `Exception.class` handler, so these now render as `application/problem+json` with unchanged HTTP status codes. Logic stayed where it belongs: the null-city contract in the resolver, HTTP problem rendering in the advice, and business-rule decisions in the controller.",
